ng  but as you go down the tail of possible  risks you're getting into potentially  infinitesimal risks in which case there  is no system conceivable or existing  that will have zero risk of every  possibility if we could do that if we  had some serious theory by which we  could say okay we've got within epsilon  of the true human  ranking over futures I think that's very  hard to do we literally do not have a  clue how and the purpose of these  examples is actually to dismiss the idea  that this has a simple solution so  people want to dismiss the idea of risk  by saying oh we'll just give the AI  system such-and-such objective and then  the failure mode goes away and  everything's cool and then people say oh  but no if you look if you give it the  objective that everyone should be happy  then here's a solution that the AI  system could find that clearly we  wouldn't want those protesters our lead  actually to deeper questioning what do  we really mean by happy we don't just  mean pleasure as measured by the  pleasure center in the brain and the  same arguments happened in moral  philosophy so no one is accusing GE  Moore of being a naive idiot because he  objected to a pleasure maximization  definition of what is a good moral  decision to make he was making an  important philosophical point and I  don't think we should dismiss that same  point when it's made in the context of  designing objectives her AI systems  however I don't think we have to worry  about a universally empowered AI system  has just given the goal of maximizing  human happiness that's the straw man and  particularly that in the scenarios that  are designed to so worry such as  conscripting the entire human race is  involuntary guinea pigs and cancer  experiments even if there is an epsilon  that we can't go below in laying out  possible risks doesn't strike me that  that's within the epsilon  these are strike me more as exercises of  human imagination  assuming a ridiculous waste simplified  sys